  "response": "Fermi's Gamma-ray Burst Monitor independently triggered on a short gamma-ray burst designated GRB 170817A, an alert generated by an instrument operating on an entirely different detection principle. The burst arrived 1.7 seconds after the gravitational-wave merger time, and so small an offset after a journey of tens of megaparsecs shows that the measured delay between the gravitational-wave and gamma-ray arrivals constrained the speed of gravity to within a few parts in 10^15 of the speed of light. The timing information thus did double duty, tying the two signals to one astrophysical event and converting that association into a quantitative test of fundamental physics.\n\nSky position supplied the second constraint. The Fermi-GBM localization region for GRB 170817A overlapped the gravitational-wave sky map, and that concordance in direction shows that the joint temporal and spatial analysis yielded a chance coincidence probability of 5 x 10^-8. The joint analysis of the gamma-ray and gravitational-wave data gave a chance coincidence probability of about one in one thousand; a one-in-a-thousand accidental rate and a rate of 5 x 10^-8 cannot both characterize the same pair of signals, so one of the two figures has to be given up. On the stronger number, an accidental pairing of an unrelated burst with an unrelated merger is excluded for all practical purposes.\n\nOptical follow-up completed the chain of identification. The Swope telescope identified the optical transient SSS17a in NGC 4993 about 11 hours after the merger. The optical counterpart of GW170817 was first identified by the Hubble Space Telescope, a priority that the Swope detection of SSS17a at the head of the search sequence rules out. That the transient was found inside NGC 4993 shows that the host galaxy lies about 40 megaparsecs from Earth; in other words, NGC 4993 is located roughly 130 million light-years away. Such proximity, set against the unusually small gamma-ray fluence recorded, indicates that the gamma-ray emission of GRB 170817A was produced by a relativistic jet viewed off its axis. At least one of two mechanisms generated the observed gamma rays, and perhaps both did: the off-axis relativistic jet, or a shock breaking out of a mildly relativistic cocoon surrounding it.\n\nEstablishing a common origin for GW170817 and GRB 170817A rested on several independent lines of measurement, each of which had to be consistent with a single merging binary. The LIGO Hanford and LIGO Livingston detectors both registered the GW170817 signal, so the event was secured in two widely separated interferometers. The gravitational-wave waveform matched the inspiral of two compact objects with masses consistent with neutron stars, which fixed the physical character of the source system.",
